\ A concrete `pick-n` that buries a quotation, a `swap` that brings it back to
\ the top, then a `call`. This is the `(try-rules)` pre-call shuffle
\ (`2 pick-n swap call`): pick a value up from depth 2, swap the quotation back
\ to the top, call it against the picked value. The `pick-n` leaves the abstract
\ stack holding a cross-slot reference (the picked value lives at a physical slot
\ above where the quotation sat), so the `call` must flush that rearrangement to
\ physical memory before invoking the quotation. The compiled `call` emits the
\ flush twice -- once for the interpreter-fallback branch and once for the
\ direct-code-ptr branch -- and each branch must flush from the same pre-flush
\ state. Built under runtime-image AOT, so these results come from the compiled
\ path and match the interpreter.

\ Literal quotation. stack: 100 200 [dup *]; `2 pick-n` copies the 100 over the
\ quotation, `swap` puts the quotation back on top, `call` runs `dup *` on 100.
100 200 [ dup * ] 2 pick-n swap call
. . .
"--" print-line

\ Quotation fetched from an array at runtime (a genuine Value slot rather than a
\ compile-time literal body), same `pick-n swap call` shape. Mirrors
\ `(try-rules)`'s `match: @get ... call`.
100 200 { [ dup * ] } 0 #nth 2 pick-n swap call
. . .
